├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ fix                                                                                       │
│ patch_name               │ trie_safe_reorder                                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ The original implementation failed validation because it changed and corrupted dataframe  │
│                          │ structure while recursively rebuilding rows. In particular, it added an `original_index`  │
│                          │ data column, merged/split frames, wrote rows using completion order from `as_completed`,  │
│                          │ dropped/restored columns inconsistently, and finally returned an empty `column_orderings` │
│                          │ list. This could lose row identity, misalign values with rows, and violate the required   │
│                          │ per-row ordering contract.                                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The replacement keeps every input cell, row, index, and column intact. It builds a        │
│                          │ bounded set of column-order candidates using serialized values, including a global        │
│                          │ repetition-weighted order and a conditional grouping order. Candidates are selected using │
│                          │ exact sorted-string LCP reuse, matching the Trie objective. The returned dataframe        │
│                          │ contains only permutations of original row cells, and `column_orderings` describes the    │
│                          │ source-column order for every returned row.                                               │
